{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"INVESTIGATION: premise REFUTED — this is not a concurrency-group collision. Not claiming; reporting back for a scope decision.\n\n## What the evidence actually shows\n\nThe cancelled job in run 31560638673 (change 1615 ps2) is a single job, 'mutation (changed behavioral shards on the patchset) / bounded mutation gate'. Its GitHub check-run annotations read:\n\n    The job has exceeded the maximum execution time of 30m0s\n    The operation was canceled.\n\nTimings: started 03:39:25, completed 04:09:41 = 30m16s. The identical pattern appears on run 31560592163 (change 1620 ps2): 03:38:14 -> 04:08:29 = 30m15s, same two annotations.\n\nThe 04:09:41 coincidence with run 31562353593 (change 1624 ps2) being created is exactly that — a coincidence. 04:09:41 is 30m00s after the job started; the job died on its own clock, not because another change's run pre-empted it.\n\n## Why the concurrency-group theory does not hold\n\n1. .github/workflows/_mutation.yml pins 'timeout-minutes: 30' on the 'bounded mutation gate' job. That is the documented cause GitHub annotated.\n2. Only ONE job was cancelled in each run; all 14 others completed. A workflow-level concurrency cancel terminates every in-flight job in the run, not one.\n3. The concurrency group is ALREADY keyed per change: gerrit-verify-GERRIT_CHANGE_ID with a run_id fallback. GERRIT_CHANGE_ID is a required workflow_dispatch input, so it resolves; distinct changes get distinct groups.\n4. The cross-change cancellations I could actually attribute were all same-change supersedes, which is intended: 1615/1 superseded by 1615/2, 1620/1 by 1620/2, and 1618/2 (03:56, run 31561628712) by 1618/2 (03:59, run 31561809295, which then succeeded).\n\nSo AC1 and AC2 are already satisfied by the current key, and AC3 cannot be reproduced because the behaviour it describes does not occur.\n\n## The real defect\n\nThe bounded mutation gate does not fit inside its own 30-minute budget on a Gerrit patchset. When it blows the timeout, the job conclusion is 'cancelled'; gerrit-verify.yaml's vote job aggregates run conclusions (mutation is in its needs list) and casts Verified -1 with a 'CANCELLED:' message that carries no test signal. A recheck just spends another 30 minutes and hits the same wall, which is the starvation the ticket describes — the symptom is real, the mechanism is not.\n\nOnly AC4 survives intact and is worth keeping: a cancellation/timeout is not a test verdict and should not read as a bare Verified -1.\n\n## Options (needs a decision — each changes CI behaviour beyond this ticket's stated scope)\n\na) Raise or remove timeout-minutes: 30 on the mutation job.\nb) Tighten the gate so a shard finishes inside the budget (shard the selection further / bound mutants per shard).\nc) Distinguish timeout from failure in the vote step so a timed-out run does not present as an unexplained CANCELLED -1 (this is AC4).\n\n(b)+(c) look like the right pair, but that is a materially different change from 're-key the concurrency group', so I stopped rather than implement it under this ticket."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786510825260152001,"uuid":"2d6a84e2-8e61-4a04-8886-fc2438e8ead2"}